{
  "typography": {
    "$description": "DTCG `typography` COMPOSITES — the Figma-facing view of the type ramp. One composite per role; each member references the CSS-facing sub-tokens in semantic/scale (type.*) plus a Figma family name (font-figma.*). The TOKEN BRIDGE expands each composite into individual bindable variables (Figma-native units: line-height/letter-spacing as %, weight/size as FLOAT, family/style as STRING) in a `type` variable collection, then creates one Figma TEXT STYLE per role bound to them. The set stays `disabled` in every $theme so Style Dictionary never EMITS it to CSS; since the chrome ramp landed it is nonetheless LOADED as source (scripts/build-tokens.mjs, SOURCE_ONLY), because a component token now binds a whole composite — stamp's text element is one ref to typography.badge — and the expansion into five CSS custom properties can only resolve if the composite is in the dictionary. fontStyle mirrors fontWeight (Regular/Bold) and is dropped from the CSS emit: it names a font FILE, not a CSS font-style. fontFamily still names the Figma face for every role, chrome included — the CSS build swaps that name for the skin's var() stack on its way in (build-tokens.mjs, figma-family-to-css-stack), so one convention serves both readers. Fluid sizes (display, heading-1) are pinned to their clamp() DESKTOP MAX by the bridge and are pull-only until the Desktop/Mobile mode axis lands.",
    "display": {
      "$type": "typography",
      "$value": {
        "fontFamily": "{font-figma.display}",
        "fontSize": "{type.display.size}",
        "fontWeight": "{type.display.weight}",
        "lineHeight": "{type.display.leading}",
        "letterSpacing": "{type.display.tracking}",
        "fontStyle": "Regular"
      }
    },
